wasm Linux cannot interrupt userspace: the scheduler and signal delivery run
only when a task enters the kernel. Hush happened to call waitpid() for each
command, but ash runs a loop of builtins with no syscalls at all, so
`sh -c 'while :; do :; done' &` held its CPU until a reboot and could not be
killed. ash now makes one cheap syscall per evaluated node. getppid() is
used because its entry polls the timer deadline (driving the tick and
preemption) and its exit runs pending signals; sched_yield() would give the
CPU away on every builtin regardless of the timeslice.

diff --git a/shell/ash.c b/shell/ash.c
index 02a665f..b710c14 100644
--- a/shell/ash.c
+++ b/shell/ash.c
@@ -9415,6 +9415,14 @@ evaltree(union node *n, int flags)
 	}
 	TRACE(("evaltree(%p: %d, %d) called\n", n, n->type, flags));
 
+	/* wasm Linux preempts a task and delivers its signals only when it
+	 * enters the kernel. A loop of builtins makes no syscalls, so it would
+	 * hold its CPU and ignore even SIGKILL. Enter the kernel once per node,
+	 * as hush's per-command waitpid() did. The syscall's tick check lets
+	 * the scheduler run others when the slice is up, and a signal it
+	 * delivers is handled by dotrap() below. */
+	getppid();
+
 	dotrap();
 
 	switch (n->type) {
